mariadb (1:10.11.8-1) unstable; urgency=medium

   CVE-2024-21096 fixes break forward and backward compatibility
   on client side. This occurs particularly during dump and
   restore of database.

   MariaDB Client now has an option --sandbox or the MariaDB client
   prompt command \-. This enables sandbox mode for the rest of the session,
   until disconnected. Once in sandbox mode, any command that could do
   something on the shell is disabled.

   The second part of the fix patch to mariadb-dump which adds a
   command right at the very top of the dump, in a comment,
   to trigger sandbox mode. It looks like this:

   /*!999999\- enable the sandbox mode */

   Newer version of MariaDB clients strip this down to the backslash
   and dash (\-), and then try to execute the internal command
   with a dash.

   Older versions of MariaDB client and all versions of MySQL client
   do not understand this command, and they will error on it.

   If you are importing a dump from a new MariaDB client version into
   a version that has not had this fix, or any version of MySQL,
   that line will error. Other methods of importing into MariaDB Server,
   such as using a language’s database connector, will be fine.

   Note that, this error could occurs for restoring old dump (backup),
   rolling back the database by using dump, or during disaster
   recovery.

   Possible workaround includes:
   1. Always import the dump using the secure client from a fixed version
     (recommended)
   2. Use an older (insecure) version of mariadb-dump to take the backup
      (remotely for instance)
   3. Remove the line at backup time with something like the command:
      mariadb-dump | tail -n +2.
   4. Remove the line at import time with something like the command
      tail -n +2|mariadb.

   The last three options (2, 3, 4) expose to the risk of CVE-2024-21096, and
   a risk analysis must be carried before using it.

   We strongly recommend to use option 1. If dump is fully trusted, option 4
   might be acceptable for disaster recovery purpose but will likely need
   to take compensatory measures.

 -- Otto Kekäläinen <otto@debian.org>  Tue, 06 Aug 2024 22:11:24 +0000

